.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt LOCAL-CONSOLE 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm local-console
.Nd managed local login, retained text sessions, and kernel maintenance
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The final served operator image is built with
.Ev ABSD_LOCAL_CONSOLE=1
by default and
provides two local text sessions without requiring a network interface or MWM.
Startup evidence appears before the login view. Input before the prompt is
discarded, except that Ctrl-] k can select read-only kernel maintenance.
Local presentation does not establish a network listener.
After handoff, ordinary service output remains available through
.Xr logs 1 ;
the raw serial worker capture is an incomplete startup prefix, without a
complete-stream END record. Non-local boots retain full serial framing.
.Pp
At
.Ql operator token:
type the enrolled 32-character token and Enter.
Input is not echoed.
Missing or malformed enrollment disables shell login; there is no default secret.
The local identity has the same filesystem and session-control rights as the SSH operator.
The verifier and SSH keys under
.Pa /ssh
are excluded from session mounts.
.Pp
Press and release Ctrl-], then one ordinary key:
.Bl -tag -width "1 or 2"
.It 1 or 2
Switch to the selected retained session, logging in independently if necessary.
.It l
Ask to log out of the selected session.
Press Enter to confirm; any other key cancels.
Confirmation clears its input and retained transcript.
.It k
Select read-only kernel maintenance, handled directly by init.
Repeat to start another kernel snapshot.
.It r
Return to the selected login or shell and replay its retained transcript.
.El
.Pp
For an authenticated shell, an unrecognized selector is passed to ordinary
cooked input as literal Ctrl-] followed by that byte. If a logout request is
cancelled by an ordinary byte, the literal prefix, l, and that byte are
restored together. A second Ctrl-] during logout confirmation is also
restored literally; it does not start another console prefix.
At the login prompt, restoration does not apply: the pending prefix is
dropped and only the current byte is processed as token input.
.Pp
Valid selectors still consume their byte even after a prefix received in an
earlier input batch. For example, Ctrl-] followed by read consumes r to
redraw and leaves ead as shell input. Use the prefix only when selecting a
console action.
Ctrl-C cancels a pending prefix or logout request and interrupts normally.
.Pp
Only the selected session receives input.
Each retains 16 KiB of output; gaps are reported.
Backspace and DEL erase a character; Ctrl-U erases the input line.
Ctrl-C discards the input line, complete lines still queued by the local
producer, and the remaining input in that batch. Previously delivered bytes
are not retracted. It interrupts the current foreground command
through the shell's existing session-control channel.
Ctrl-D submits partial text, or closes stdin when the input line is empty.
A shell exit or fault ends that session and requires fresh authentication.
Failed authentication and resource refusal apply bounded delays, up to 30 seconds.
There is no automatic shell respawn.
.Pp
The local producer supports plain pause and discard recovery, without a
staged paste record protocol.
Pasted bursts use ordinary cooked lines, including when bracketed-paste
delimiters are present; each newline can submit a command.
There is no staged paste approval.
The pending input queue is bounded to 8192 bytes; a full queue reports a
dropped line. A line longer than 65536 bytes or invalid UTF-8 refuses input.
The refused command and its pending suffix are discarded until an explicit
Ctrl-C. The session survives; enter a separate command after its fresh prompt.
While input is refused, newlines, paste delimiters and Ctrl-] do not release it.
The shell drains inherited input only while the producer has acknowledged a
pause, and the producer rearms only after its pending input has been discarded.
.Pp
The local text terminal uses
.Ev TERM=dumb .
Dimensions come from an actual renderer or serial-client observation;
unknown dimensions remain unknown. A fixed framebuffer can report 80 by 25,
but no such size is invented for an unmeasured serial terminal.
Use
.Xr terminal-size 1
to inspect the current size. COLUMNS and LINES are snapshots refreshed at
shell prompts and native command startup when dimensions are known.
There is no raw mode, process-group job control or detached-job support.
Commands cannot change terminal modes; shell end resets the line discipline.
.Sh RECOVERY
The kernel view requires no file server, logger, NIC, SSH listener or compositor.
Physical Console possession grants only diagnostic reading, not a shell or reboot.
Essential service failure triggers bounded cleanup. After readiness, the
advertised recovery budget may permit a visible whole-machine reset.
Before readiness, on unknown or exhausted budget, or if reset is refused,
a local-enabled image holds read-only maintenance.
An external operator reboot is required to leave that held state;
a dirty writable image may also require offline recovery.
Neither reset nor maintenance is an in-place service repair.
The headless image retains its bounded recovery-reset and stop policy.
.Sh ENROLLMENT
Fresh operator and release builds create enrollment automatically.
The secret is stored on the build host at
.Pa OUT.local-console-token
and its reusable descriptor at
.Pa OUT.local-console-token.verifier ,
both mode 0600 and outside the artifact output directory.
Neither file is printed or copied into the artifact directory.
Only the descriptor bytes are imported as
.Pa /ssh/local-login .
Keep the token under operator custody and transfer it separately from images.
Use
.Fl -local-token-file Ar path
to choose a different external destination, or
.Fl -local-login-verifier Ar file
to reuse an enrollment when rebuilding.
An existing enrollment is never overwritten; token destinations inside the
artifact directory and raw tokens supplied as descriptors are refused.
Importer kernels explicitly use
.Ev ABSD_LOCAL_CONSOLE=0 ;
only the final served kernel enables local input and maintenance.
.Pp
For manual enrollment on the build host use
.Pa scripts/local-console-enroll.py
with
.Fl -verifier Ar file
and
.Fl -token-file Ar file .
It creates a random 128-bit token and SHA-256 verifier in separate mode-0600 files.
Install only the verifier at
.Pa /ssh/local-login
using the existing image import/defaults path.
Keep the token outside the image under operator custody.
.Pp
The console service emits its readiness record on supervised standard
output. For an enrolled console, init uses that bound stream to retire
bootstrap frame admission.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Unterminated local input that accumulates past 1 MiB refuses the unfinished
command and discards pending input until Ctrl-C, then offers a fresh prompt.
SSH without a PTY still ends the shell session at that source limit.
.Pp
A forced session END can leave adopted processes whose filesystem binding
has been revoked; this is not a process-tree termination guarantee.
Physical hardware_fixture input and display operation require a separate observed hardware run.
Serial QEMU tests do not establish physical usability.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr dmesg 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr terminal-size 1 ,
